WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.360
- The question the community
has is, does this work?

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.097
If you believe that it does,
I'd like to hear you say that.

00:00:05.097 --> 00:00:06.270
- I know it does.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.570
- [Narrator] This is Elizabeth Holmes.

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:11.990
Six years ago, she was one
of the most celebrated CEOs

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:13.210
in Silicon Valley.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.070
Her biotech startup Theranos,

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.530
which promised to
revolutionize blood testing

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.863
was once valued at more than $9 billion.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.010
And Forbes held her

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:25.095
as the youngest self-made
female billionaire.

00:00:25.095 --> 00:00:27.700
But then Theranos's well-crafted story

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.620
began to fall apart

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.910
just days before Holmes took to the stage

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.107
at this 2015 conference.

00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:36.400
"The Wall Street Journal"
published two articles

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.410
that cast serious doubts
on the effectiveness

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.810
of Theranos's technology.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.605
- Do you feel now, in hindsight that

00:00:43.605 --> 00:00:46.390
maybe you went to market
a little too quickly?

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.510
- We're the exact same company
that we were last Wednesday

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:51.660
before these two articles were published,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.180
nothing has changed.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.540
Just because some guy
reports false stuff about us

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.280
doesn't mean that it changes our business.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.410
- We knew she was blatantly prevaricating.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.350
And it's interesting that the government

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.870
has used many of the facts that
we uncovered in our stories

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.330
as a basis for the criminal complaint

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.850
that's now pending against Elizabeth.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.729
- [Narrator] Today
Theranos has been dissolved

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:13.000
and Holmes faces a federal indictment

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.397
of 12 different charges,

00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:17.078
two counts of conspiracy
to commit wire fraud

00:01:17.078 --> 00:01:20.000
and 10 counts of wire fraud.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.200
- The case has two parts.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.680
The prosecutors alleged she
defrauded both investors

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.810
who gave hundreds of millions
of dollars to Theranos

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.370
and also patients who paid for tests.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.430
- [Narrator] Now with
her trial about to begin

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:33.089
on August 31st, we
revisit Theranos's story

00:01:33.089 --> 00:01:35.161
and Holmes's fall from grace.

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.840
To understand what we
can expect from a case

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.742
that is shaping up to be
one of the biggest trials

00:01:39.742 --> 00:01:41.453
in recent years.

00:01:41.453 --> 00:01:43.360
- The woman who I will be interviewing

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.440
needs no introduction.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.740
- First, they think you're
crazy, then they fight you

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.670
and then all of a sudden
you change the world.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.410
- [Reporter] Almost every
media outlet, including us,

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.600
here at CBS bought into the Theranos myth.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:55.740
- Breaking news right now,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.177
Elizabeth Holmes is stepping
down as CEO of Theranos.

00:01:59.177 --> 00:02:00.419
- [Narrator] The Theranos saga

00:02:00.419 --> 00:02:03.340
has been one of Silicon
Valley's biggest scandals,

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.210
and as such, it's attracted
a lot of attention.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.650
To date, the Theranos
story has been chronicled

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:12.350
in a best-selling book, an
HBO documentary, a podcast,

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.230
and two still to come
Hollywood adaptations.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:16.400
- Before "The Journal's" stories,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.420
Elizabeth was a darling of Silicon Valley.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.872
She was, you know, viewed as

00:02:21.872 --> 00:02:24.180
potentially the next Steve Jobs.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.960
- [Narrator] Theranos
promised to revolutionize

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.805
blood testing with a new technology

00:02:27.805 --> 00:02:31.577
that could diagnose everything
from cholesterol to HIV

00:02:31.577 --> 00:02:34.420
using only a few drops of blood.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.692
- We'd like to see a world
in which every person

00:02:36.692 --> 00:02:40.455
gets access to this type of basic testing

00:02:40.455 --> 00:02:42.050
- [Narrator] From an early age,

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.669
Elizabeth Holmes had
aspirations to be an engineer.

00:02:44.669 --> 00:02:46.874
- I first met her when
she came to my office

00:02:46.874 --> 00:02:49.230
when she was a freshman at Stanford

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.990
and I believe it was the fall of 2002.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:54.930
- [Narrator] In this deposition interview,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.511
one of Holmes' earliest backers,

00:02:56.511 --> 00:02:59.273
Stanford University
professor Channing Robertson,

00:02:59.273 --> 00:03:01.170
recall that by the next year,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.440
Holmes had decided to drop
out and launch Theranos.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.147
- She asked me, if I would be
on her board and I said, yes.

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:12.030
- [Narrator] Holmes
started Theranos in 2003,

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.006
after dropping out as a
19 year old sophomore.

00:03:15.006 --> 00:03:16.790
Over the next 12 years,

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.160
she raised hundreds of
millions from investors,

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:23.252
and Theranos's valuation
ballooned to more than $9 billion.

00:03:23.252 --> 00:03:25.560
Two former Secretaries of State,

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.840
Henry Kissinger and George Schultz

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.880
were members of the board of directors.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.573
- And how did you first
become involved with Theranos?

00:03:35.726 --> 00:03:40.630
- Secretary Schultz mentioned the company

00:03:44.101 --> 00:03:47.060
that he reached out shortly,

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.100
maybe several years
after I left the Senate.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.180
- [Narrator] In this deposition
taken in a civil case

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.570
brought by investors,

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:53.950
former Theranos board member

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.340
and former Senate
Majority Leader Bill Frist

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.230
recalled the first time
he met Elizabeth Holmes.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.540
- They had a box there, a machine there

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:08.160
and told the history,
her individual history

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:14.573
and the stage of the
company at that point.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:21.639
I do remember actually at that
meeting doing a finger prick,

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:24.970
the technology and the results
came back at that meeting.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.380
- So you did test the technology

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:27.670
right at that first meeting?

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:28.580
- Yes.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:30.388
- And it was analyzed
right there and then?

00:04:30.388 --> 00:04:31.221
- Yes.

00:04:31.221 --> 00:04:32.242
- And the results were given to you.

00:04:32.242 --> 00:04:33.075
- Yes.

00:04:33.075 --> 00:04:34.200
- Right there and then?

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.050
- [Narrator] Investors
like the Walton Family

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.777
and Rupert Murdoch, executive
chairman of NewsCorp,

00:04:38.777 --> 00:04:40.490
"The Journal's" parent company,

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.020
each invested over $100 million.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.690
Others like former education
secretary, Betsy Devos's family

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.800
invested $100 million.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.203
And Mexican tycoon Carlos
Slim, invested $30 million.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.880
- If this technology truly worked,

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.820
it could have uppended the
entire blood testing industry,

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.230
it could have made them a lot of money.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:03.530
- [Narrator] But after the publication

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.240
of two "Wall Street Journal"
articles in October 2015,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.720
Holmes's claims began to unravel.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.392
- You're now at the point

00:05:11.392 --> 00:05:16.392
where you're only testing
for one thing, herpes

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.460
using your proprietary
technology, is that correct?

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.338
- It's not correct.

00:05:22.338 --> 00:05:26.130
And there's a lot of
different elements of our work

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:30.987
that have been conflated
through these two pieces.

00:05:30.987 --> 00:05:32.630
- [Narrator] "The Journal"
reported that Theranos

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.450
wasn't using its own technology

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:35.757
for the vast majority of its tests

00:05:35.757 --> 00:05:39.600
and it was instead using
commercially available machines.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.140
The company's Edison machine,

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.220
the lab instrument touted as
the linchpin of its strategy

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.330
handled just a small fraction of tests

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:47.840
that were sold to consumers.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.680
And former employees told "The Journal",

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.670
the accuracy of Edison's tests was poor.

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:56.940
- So if I went to one of the
centers and I got a pinprick

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.740
and I only gave that much blood, right,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.740
what tests are you currently
able to perform for that blood

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.050
using anything other than

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:12.440
commercially available lab equipment?

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.900
- So we have never used
commercially available lab equipment

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:18.670
for finger-stick based tests.

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:21.350
Every finger stick tests
that we have ever done

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.750
uses proprietary Theranos technology

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.610
that is not commercially available.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.850
- Well, it sounded great
for her to say that

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:31.159
they never used third-party
commercially available

00:06:31.159 --> 00:06:32.920
blood testing technology.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:34.828
Unfortunately, it's not grounded in facts.

00:06:34.828 --> 00:06:37.810
- [Narrator] In this 2018
deposition interview,

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:40.630
former Theranos product
manager, Daniel Edlin,

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:42.100
recalled a company meeting

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:45.430
in which the use of third-party
analyzers was discussed.

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:50.430
- I heard discussion about
certain Theranos tests being

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:56.560
lab developed tests that were
run on third party machines.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:59.190
- [Narrator] The 2016 "Journal" article

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:02.170
found that Theranos failed
to maintain basic safeguards

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:04.030
to ensure consistent results.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:05.360
What's more because of the company's

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.050
many questionable tests,
some patients adjusted

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:09.990
the amount of medicine they were taking.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:13.380
Others like breast cancer
survivors, Sherry Eckard panicked

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:17.070
as seen here in this 2016
"Wall Street Journal" video.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.980
- When I went online and got the results,

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.880
I printed them off,

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.070
it looks like they're normal

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:25.900
and then I thought I better go
back and look at that again.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.518
Okay, what's the range,
the reference range?

00:07:28.518 --> 00:07:32.688
And I noticed that it's
for premenopausal women.

00:07:32.688 --> 00:07:34.159
Okay, well, that's not me.

00:07:34.159 --> 00:07:35.900
- [Narrator] Eckard, who could be called

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:37.370
to testify in the trial

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:39.580
received blood test results from Theranos

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.203
that showed estrogen
levels many times higher

00:07:42.203 --> 00:07:45.300
than is normal for a
post-menopausal woman.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:46.900
Her oncologist told "The Journal"

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.990
a high estrogen level
could cause cancer to recur

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:52.620
or could be a sign of a rare tumor.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:55.072
- Either they made a mistake,

00:07:55.072 --> 00:07:57.540
they tested somebody else's blood,

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.540
or their controls don't work,
or I have a tumor somewhere.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:05.590
- The patients where
the pawns in all this.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:07.060
Many of them who had gotten tests

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:09.930
and the tests seemed to be wildly off.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:13.100
They would sometimes have
repeated tests at Theranos,

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.070
and then when those were wildly off,

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:18.150
they would go to a traditional,
you know, Quest or Lab Corp.

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.210
When they complained,

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.696
oftentimes they would not
get any kind of a response.

00:08:21.696 --> 00:08:25.270
- You have no concerns
about where you are today.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:27.980
- What we've done for the last
few days is listen, right?

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:29.215
And try to understand

00:08:29.215 --> 00:08:31.320
what are the questions
that the community has,

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:33.360
and we've been preparing material

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.330
to be able to answer that for them,

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:38.438
because that matters hugely to me.

00:08:38.438 --> 00:08:41.480
- [Narrator] At this event,
Holmes promised to publish data

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:45.570
proving the accuracy of
Theranos' more than 240 tests,

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.731
but Theranos was never
able to provide that data.

00:08:48.731 --> 00:08:51.230
The records of the
millions of blood tests,

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:54.290
that Theranos performed
were stored on a database.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:58.070
But in 2018, Theranos'
employees dismantled the servers

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.910
containing the original database.

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:02.320
Meanwhile, prosecutors
didn't realize for months

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:03.486
that a copy they received

00:09:03.486 --> 00:09:06.716
was missing a necessary encryption code.

00:09:06.716 --> 00:09:09.860
- At this point, neither
the government nor Theranos

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.610
or Elizabeth Holmes have access
to this database anymore.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:15.126
- Kind of extraordinary that
it just sort of disappeared.

00:09:15.126 --> 00:09:16.960
- [Narrator] Prosecutors believe

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:18.291
that even without the database,

00:09:18.291 --> 00:09:20.546
a successful prosecution
can be carried out

00:09:20.546 --> 00:09:23.032
with the anecdotal evidence available.

00:09:23.032 --> 00:09:25.340
- Holmes's defense team has argued

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.654
that without this
database and broader data

00:09:27.654 --> 00:09:29.690
prosecutors shouldn't be able to call

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.290
patients and doctors to the stand

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.400
who said they got inaccurate test results.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:36.150
So they've put a lot of
stake in this lost database.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:38.350
- [Narrator] Prosecutors
first indicted Holmes

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:41.443
and former Theranose executive
Ramesh Sunny Balwani.

00:09:41.443 --> 00:09:45.120
In June 2018 Holmes settled civil charges

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:48.000
filed by the SEC in 2018.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.930
- Prosecutors say that from
2010 to 2015 in investor pitches

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:53.210
that Elizabeth Holmes did,

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:54.810
she basically told investors

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:56.670
that they had this
revolutionary technology,

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:58.880
but they say that in
reality, that wasn't true

00:09:58.880 --> 00:09:59.920
and she knew it.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:01.900
- [Narrator] Prosecutors
say investors were told,

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:02.970
Theranos would generate

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:06.575
over $100 million in revenue in 2014

00:10:06.575 --> 00:10:08.770
and that Theranos had lucrative contracts

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:11.810
with the Department of
Defense that didn't exist.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:13.760
In the indictment prosecutors point to

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:16.865
six specific wire transfers
from unnamed investors

00:10:16.865 --> 00:10:20.710
that they say were the result
of allegedly fraudulent acts,

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:23.223
including a nearly $100 million deposit

00:10:23.223 --> 00:10:27.920
to a Theranos account
sent in October, 2014.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.110
- The intent is what matters in showing

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:31.970
that someone intended to deceive and so

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:34.220
if Elizabeth Holmes' lawyers can say

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:36.530
that she believed in the technology,

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:38.170
that could be one line of defense.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:40.175
Elizabeth Holmes's
lawyers could also argue

00:10:40.175 --> 00:10:42.490
that also all Silicon Valley startups

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:43.710
embellish to investors

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:46.060
and that what Theranos
did was no different.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:47.010
- [Narrator] It's unclear if Holmes

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:48.510
will testify in the trial

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.318
or what the defense's strategy will be.

00:10:50.318 --> 00:10:52.730
- To me, the most interesting element,

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:54.664
is Elizabeth Holmes gonna
testify on her own behalf?

00:10:54.664 --> 00:10:59.180
I mean, it's always the big
question and I tend to doubt it,

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:01.940
but you know, it it's
unclear, we don't know that,

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:02.773
we also don't know

00:11:02.773 --> 00:11:04.658
who definitively the
witnesses are going to be.

00:11:04.658 --> 00:11:06.600
- [Narrator] Choosing an impartial jury

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.174
is expected to be a major undertaking

00:11:08.174 --> 00:11:12.100
because of the substantial
coverage Theranos has received.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:15.070
- I think it could take a while
and be an intensive process

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:15.970
to pick the jury.

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:18.220
Elizabeth Holmes's side is very concerned

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:19.930
about finding jurors that aren't

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:22.122
already prejudiced against
her and the company.

00:11:22.122 --> 00:11:24.610
- [Narrator] Holmes
has pleaded not guilty.

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:25.443
Each wire fraud count

00:11:25.443 --> 00:11:28.803
carries a maximum prison
sentence of 20 years.

00:11:29.659 --> 00:11:32.326
(upbeat music)

